WEBVTT

00:00:00.556 --> 00:00:03.906
Substitution replaces
each letter with another.

00:00:04.361 --> 00:00:05.711
The alphabet is scrambled,

00:00:06.201 --> 00:00:08.051
but the length stays the same.

00:00:10.529 --> 00:00:14.699
A substitution cipher maps plaintext
letters to ciphertext letters.

00:00:15.254 --> 00:00:16.944
The key is the mapping itself.

00:00:17.619 --> 00:00:18.969
With 26 letters

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:22.189
there are 26 factorial possible mappings.

00:00:23.922 --> 00:00:24.952
Caesar cipher:

00:00:25.382 --> 00:00:27.352
shift every letter by a fixed key.

00:00:27.857 --> 00:00:30.607
Key 3: meet becomes phhw.

00:00:31.217 --> 00:00:34.167
A becomes D, B becomes E, and so on.

00:00:35.822 --> 00:00:39.772
Monoalphabetic:
an arbitrary one to one substitution.

00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:44.077
The key is a permutation
of all 26 letters.

00:00:44.647 --> 00:00:47.297
About 10 to the 26 possible keys.

00:00:49.089 --> 00:00:51.419
Playfair: encrypts pairs of letters.

00:00:52.049 --> 00:00:54.619
Build a 5 by 5 grid from a keyword.

00:00:55.239 --> 00:00:59.349
Replace each pair using row,
column or rectangle rules.

00:01:01.122 --> 00:01:01.992
Hill cipher:

00:01:02.422 --> 00:01:07.612
treat letters as numbers and
multiply by a key matrix modulo 26.

00:01:08.297 --> 00:01:10.587
It encrypts blocks of letters at once.

00:01:12.322 --> 00:01:14.512
Substitution creates confusion.

00:01:15.187 --> 00:01:19.157
The relation between key and
ciphertext becomes complex.

00:01:19.822 --> 00:01:23.772
Shannon's property of confusion
is achieved by substitution.

